/// `rule-conditions`: Conditions on a rule.
/// Implemented: a rule's condition read over its variables, a freshness condition `where a # t`, overlaps decided under the condition they compose, and rules under a row of theories deciding overlaps in the meet of their rows.
/// Not implemented: a model owing a law under a row only where the row holds: today it owes it in every row.
///
/// - owes: #2466
/// - see: #1934
//! An atom named inside the abstraction that binds it
//! [Fernandez-2007-nominal_rewriting], § 2, and
//! [Pitts-2013-nominal_sets_names_symmetry_computer_science], § 4. Inside `bind
//! a . t` the name `a` is the atom `a`, and outside it a variable. Admission
//! re-mints an atom with the abstraction that binds it, so a rule naming the
//! channel its restriction abstracts is one rule whatever the atom is called:
//! matching, the critical pairs and the kernel's replay read an occurrence by
//! the number of abstractions between it and its binder. Where a port takes an
//! atom, a term can name one, and a rule stating a freshness condition, or
//! moving a variable across an abstraction, fires only where its instance names
//! no atom it may not, counting the atoms a moved instance names to their
//! abstractions' new places. Such a rule's variable is a hole over the atoms it
//! may name, so two rules' overlap is decided under the condition it composes.
//! A rule matching a variable both under an abstraction and beside it is not
//! decided yet: `pathological/desc/atom-occurrence.gandr`.
//!
//! - cites: [Fernandez-2007-nominal_rewriting]
//! - cites: [Pitts-2013-nominal_sets_names_symmetry_computer_science]
//!
//! [Fernandez-2007-nominal_rewriting]: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ic.2006.12.002 "Fernández and Gabbay, Nominal rewriting"
//! [Pitts-2013-nominal_sets_names_symmetry_computer_science]: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139084673 "Pitts, Nominal Sets: Names and Symmetry in Computer Science"

/// A send on a restricted channel, which no process outside the restriction
/// can receive: the process never moves, so it is the inert one. The checker
/// orients the law and the kernel replays the verdict; a restriction naming
/// its channel `b` normalizes by the law written over `a`.
@[ normalizes("nu(bind b . out(b, nil)) ~> nil") ]
theory Unheard where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule unheard : nu(bind a . out(a, p)) ==> nil();
}

/// The law below a second restriction, sending on the outer channel: the
/// occurrence counts past one abstraction to its binder and the law's counts
/// past none, so the law does not apply; the normal form names each atom by
/// its depth.
@[ normalizes("nu(bind a . nu(bind b . out(a, nil))) ~> nu(bind a0 . nu(bind a1 . out(a0, nil)))") ]
theory Overheard where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule unheard : nu(bind a . out(a, p)) ==> nil();
}

/// An inner restriction of the outer one's name shadows it: the channel sent
/// on is the inner one, and the law applies below the outer restriction.
@[ normalizes("nu(bind a . nu(bind a . out(a, nil))) ~> nu(bind a0 . nil)") ]
theory Shadowed where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule unheard : nu(bind a . out(a, p)) ==> nil();
}

/// Scope extrusion where `out` takes a channel: `q` moves under the
/// restriction stated fresh for it, so the rule counts each channel `q` names
/// one abstraction further, and the outer channel stays the one sent on. The
/// kernel replays the verdict.
@[ normalizes("nu(bind c . par(nu(bind b . out(b, nil)), out(c, nil))) ~> nu(bind a0 . nu(bind a1 . par(out(a1, nil), out(a0, nil))))") ]
theory Extruding where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper par : (p : Proc, q : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule scope : par(nu(bind a . p), q) <=> nu(bind a . par(p, q)) where { | a # q };
}

/// Garbage collection where `out` takes a channel: a restriction whose process
/// names none of its channel is the process. The rule fires on the inner
/// restriction, counting the outer channel one abstraction nearer, and not on
/// the outer one, whose process sends on it.
@[ normalizes("nu(bind c . nu(bind b . out(c, nil))) ~> nu(bind a0 . out(a0, nil))") ]
theory Collected where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule gc : nu(bind a . p) <=> p where { | a # p };
}

/// Scope extrusion beside garbage collection where `out` takes a channel
/// [Milner-1999-communicating_and_mobile_systems]. Collection rewrites the
/// restriction extrusion matches, and the overlap is decided as a higher-order
/// critical pair [Mayr-1998-higher_order_rewrite_systems_and_their_confluence],
/// each variable a pattern over the channels it may name
/// [Miller-1991-a_logic_programming_language_with_lambda_abstraction]:
/// extrusion's `p` may name the restriction's channel and its `q` may not,
/// collection's `p` may not. Composed, the peak restricts a channel neither
/// process names, and both rewrites reach the two side by side.
///
/// - cites: [Mayr-1998-higher_order_rewrite_systems_and_their_confluence]
/// - cites: [Miller-1991-a_logic_programming_language_with_lambda_abstraction]
/// - cites: [Milner-1999-communicating_and_mobile_systems]
///
/// [Mayr-1998-higher_order_rewrite_systems_and_their_confluence]: https://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3975(97)00143-6 "Mayr and Nipkow, Higher-order rewrite systems and their confluence"
/// [Miller-1991-a_logic_programming_language_with_lambda_abstraction]: https://doi.org/10.1093/logcom/1.4.497 "Miller, A Logic Programming Language with Lambda-Abstraction, Function Variables, and Simple Unification"
/// [Milner-1999-communicating_and_mobile_systems]: https://openlibrary.org/works/OL7950513W "Milner, Communicating and Mobile Systems: the π-Calculus"
@[ normalizes("nu(bind c . par(nu(bind b . nil), out(c, nil))) ~> nu(bind a0 . par(nil, out(a0, nil)))") ]
theory Collecting where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper par : (p : Proc, q : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule scope : par(nu(bind a . p), q) <=> nu(bind a . par(p, q)) where { | a # q };
  rule gc : nu(bind a . p) <=> p where { | a # p };
}

/// Garbage collection beside the law that a restriction sending only on its
/// own channel is inert: their left sides unify as terms, but collection's `p`
/// may not name the channel the law's process sends on, so no instance
/// overlaps and the pair holds without a peak.
@[ normalizes("nu(bind c . nu(bind b . out(b, nil))) ~> nil") ]
theory Quiet where {
  sort Name : Atom;
  sort Proc : Type;
  oper nil : () --> Proc;
  oper out : (c : Name, p : Proc) --> Proc;
  oper nu : (p : bind (a : Name) . Proc) --> Proc;
  rule gc : nu(bind a . p) <=> p where { | a # p };
  rule quiet : nu(bind a . out(a, nil())) <=> nil();
}
